WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.830
A district report said: in Class Six B,

2
00:00:04.270 --> 00:00:07.290
one pupil has a disability and failed

3
00:00:07.290 --> 00:00:07.710
maths.

4
00:00:08.460 --> 00:00:10.540
There is one pupil with a

5
00:00:10.540 --> 00:00:12.220
disability in Six B.

6
00:00:12.910 --> 00:00:13.970
Everyone knew who.

7
00:00:15.130 --> 00:00:18.090
Today: who may see what, and how to

8
00:00:18.090 --> 00:00:20.230
share data without exposing a child.

9
00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:22.600
Start with roles.

10
00:00:23.410 --> 00:00:25.450
A class teacher sees her own class.

11
00:00:26.140 --> 00:00:28.340
The head sees the whole school.

12
00:00:29.030 --> 00:00:30.750
Parents see their own child.

13
00:00:31.470 --> 00:00:33.170
The district sees summaries.

14
00:00:33.890 --> 00:00:35.730
Each role gets only what it

15
00:00:35.730 --> 00:00:37.750
needs: that's least privilege.

16
00:00:39.160 --> 00:00:41.360
Share summaries, not rows,

17
00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:42.440
whenever you can.

18
00:00:43.090 --> 00:00:45.590
A class average answers the district's

19
00:00:45.590 --> 00:00:47.550
question without a single name.

20
00:00:48.220 --> 00:00:50.460
But small groups give people away.

21
00:00:51.680 --> 00:00:53.740
So hide any group of fewer

22
00:00:53.740 --> 00:00:54.900
than five pupils.

23
00:00:55.660 --> 00:00:58.680
One pupil with a disability in Six B is

24
00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:00.960
not a statistic; it's a person.

25
00:01:01.610 --> 00:01:04.410
Report it at school level, or not at all.

26
00:01:05.550 --> 00:01:07.290
Replacing names with numbers

27
00:01:07.290 --> 00:01:09.790
is a start, not the end.

28
00:01:10.430 --> 00:01:12.090
It's called pseudonymisation:

29
00:01:12.670 --> 00:01:14.430
whoever holds the list of numbers

30
00:01:14.430 --> 00:01:16.010
can bring the names back.

31
00:01:16.660 --> 00:01:19.500
And a few ordinary facts can do it too.

32
00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:24.520
In a well-known study, birth date, sex and

33
00:01:24.520 --> 00:01:27.380
postcode alone picked out eighty-seven

34
00:01:27.380 --> 00:01:29.720
people in a hundred in the United States.

35
00:01:30.490 --> 00:01:34.510
In a school, a class, an age and

36
00:01:34.510 --> 00:01:36.090
one detail can be enough.

37
00:01:37.390 --> 00:01:37.870
Quick check!

38
00:01:38.510 --> 00:01:40.250
The district asks for this

39
00:01:40.250 --> 00:01:41.510
term's maths results.

40
00:01:42.270 --> 00:01:43.590
Which is safest to send?

41
00:01:44.840 --> 00:01:46.740
Averages per class, with

42
00:01:46.740 --> 00:01:48.060
groups under five hidden.

43
00:01:48.710 --> 00:01:50.870
Numbers instead of names still

44
00:01:50.870 --> 00:01:53.730
describe each child, and anyone with

45
00:01:53.730 --> 00:01:55.170
the list can match them back.

46
00:01:56.370 --> 00:01:59.210
In SQL, GROUP BY makes the averages,

47
00:01:59.750 --> 00:02:02.230
and HAVING drops the small groups.

48
00:02:02.910 --> 00:02:04.670
One line of HAVING protects

49
00:02:04.670 --> 00:02:06.570
every child in a small class.

50
00:02:07.900 --> 00:02:11.120
In the app, six harmless posts by Lina,

51
00:02:11.320 --> 00:02:14.460
fourteen, add up to her school, her route

52
00:02:14.460 --> 00:02:16.660
home and when her house is empty.

53
00:02:17.330 --> 00:02:19.490
Choose who sees each one.

54
00:02:20.130 --> 00:02:22.570
School data works the same way: each

55
00:02:22.570 --> 00:02:24.570
column alone is harmless, but

56
00:02:24.570 --> 00:02:26.530
together they point at a child.

57
00:02:27.820 --> 00:02:28.340
Your turn.

58
00:02:28.970 --> 00:02:31.070
We replaced every name with a

59
00:02:31.070 --> 00:02:32.810
number before sharing a file.

60
00:02:33.410 --> 00:02:35.410
Is the data anonymous now?

61
00:02:36.620 --> 00:02:40.080
No: it's pseudonymised, and the list or a

62
00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:42.000
few facts can bring the child back.

63
00:02:42.660 --> 00:02:44.760
Remember: each role sees

64
00:02:44.760 --> 00:02:46.060
only what it needs.

65
00:02:46.830 --> 00:02:48.890
Share summaries, and hide

66
00:02:48.890 --> 00:02:50.210
groups under five.

67
00:02:50.940 --> 00:02:52.700
And numbers instead of names

68
00:02:52.700 --> 00:02:54.980
are a start, not the end.

69
00:02:56.170 --> 00:02:58.430
Try it in class tomorrow: play

70
00:02:58.430 --> 00:03:00.650
“Guess who” with a made-up pupil.

71
00:03:01.280 --> 00:03:05.420
Give one fact at a time: her class, her

72
00:03:05.420 --> 00:03:08.740
age, her bus stop, her sports team.

73
00:03:09.210 --> 00:03:11.850
After how many facts can the

74
00:03:11.850 --> 00:03:13.490
class tell who she is?
